 :wave-1-float-surface
 ;; Measured 2026-09-09 against amu 6ffc1d71, after two of the ten wave-1
 ;; pilots were built as whole components and executed (com-aave, com-aadhaar).
 ;; Recorded here because it is a property of the TRANCHE, not of either repo:
 ;; the same generated actor template carries it, and the count decides how
 ;; much of wave-1 one backend gap holds up.
 {:what-was-counted
  "Across the ten wave-1 pilots' main.cljc: entities, fields coerced :float,
   and float fields that are also :required. Counted, not sampled."
  :float-reaching-pilots 7
  :float-on-a-required-field 4
  :per-repo
  {:com-acorrd-standards "7 float fields, 6 of them required -- the outlier"
   :com-adobe            "3 float fields, 2 required"
   :com-aadhaar          "1 float field, required (Transaction/:amount)"
   :com-aftership        "1 float field, required"
   :com-abb-robotics     "1 float field, not required"
   :com-acquia           "1 float field, not required"
   :com-aave             "no float fields -- which is why its blocked as-float
                          arm was unreachable and easy to under-rate"
   :float-free "com-aave, com-accela, com-accelbyte, com-ach-network"}

  :the-gap-is-the-native-backend-and-only-that
  ;; The decisive measurement, and it is an execution rather than a build.
  ;; `aadhaar.main/as-float` was recorded blocked on the strength of one
  ;; target. The same probe across four:
  ;;
  ;;   wasm32-browser   exit 0
  ;;   js-browser       exit 0
  ;;   aarch64-macos    exit 70
  ;;   x86_64-linux     exit 70
  ;;
  ;;   "typed values currently require the kotoba-script web target, typed
  ;;    Wasm target, or qualified native string/scalar-record/option-i64/
  ;;    result-i64 features"
  ;;
  ;; and the wasm artifact was RUN, not merely built: 2,113 bytes through
  ;; runtime/browser-host.mjs, `main` = (f64-to-i64-truncating (as-float
  ;; "12.5")) answering 12. The refusal message names its own condition --
  ;; native needs a QUALIFIED feature, and f64 does not have one yet.
  {:probe "com-aadhaar q9-probes/g23-f64.kotoba"
   :wasm32-browser :compiles-and-runs
   :js-browser :compiles
   :aarch64-macos :refused-exit-70
   :x86_64-linux :refused-exit-70
   :conclusion
   "`:float` is not a language gap and not a whole-tranche gap. It is one
    backend's missing qualification. Seven of ten pilots reach it; four reach
    it on a required field; and all ten can carry it today on the Wasm and JS
    targets."}

  :what-this-does-not-say
  "It does not say wave-1 can be qualified on Wasm. Measured the same day: a
   module declaring TWO OR MORE capabilities fails `compile --target
   wasm32-browser` with an internal compiler error, while one capability and
   both native targets are fine -- ClojureScript's `compare` cannot order the
   BigInt the wire ids arrive as on that route. Both whole components built so
   far declare two. So the Wasm route is open for the float arm and closed for
   the components that need it, and the two are independent defects that
   happen to intersect exactly here."}

 :blocked-components
 ;; ADR-q9: "If the compiler cannot express it, the migration is :blocked. The
 ;; component is not reduced to a smaller predicate to make the gate green;
 ;; the gap goes on the language surface plan." This key is that list.
 {:kotoba-set
  {:repositories ["set" "set-difference" "set-index" "set-intersection"
                  "set-join" "set-project" "set-rename" "set-rename-keys"
                  "set-select" "set-subset" "set-superset" "set-union"]
   :status :blocked
   :as-of "2026-09-11"
   :measured-on "amu b57284d6 / kotoba-sema 0470c524, amu check --jvm-free"
   ;; Same day, later: the LANGUAGE-LEVEL half of this blocker closed. Option C
   ;; landed -- guest-grammar.edn :core-form-shapes :ns (:params [...]) and
   ;; :require-spec :with, amu 5cc4d826 (PR #932) instantiating a template once
   ;; per binding -- and was re-measured at amu main: one kotoba.set.union
   ;; template at [:set :i64] and [:set :symbol] compiles to the same wasm bytes
   ;; as two hand-written modules and runs to the right answer. So these twelve
   ;; can now be WRITTEN once. They are not yet written, and :status stays
   ;; :blocked for the second blocker below, which the spelling does not touch.
   :status-update {:as-of "2026-09-11"
                   :language-level :unblocked
                   :by "module type parameters (lang/surface-status.edn :other-gaps :module-type-parameters)"
                   :templates-written 0
                   :still-blocked-by :bounded-typed-sets
                   :next "write kotoba.set.union as a template and instantiate it from one real caller that needs <=32 elements; datalog-sized callers wait on the bound"}
   :blocker :element-type-polymorphism
   :blocker-record "kotoba-lang lang/surface-status.edn :other-gaps :element-type-polymorphism"
   :why
   "These mirror clojure.set. There is no type variable in the surface, so a
    parameter is annotated with a concrete value type or defaults to :i64 --
    measured, `(defn f [s] (count s))` is refused `count requires a bounded
    vector, a typed set or a canonical typed map; got :i64`, and the same
    function annotated `[:set :i64]` is admitted. So the blocker is the TYPE
    and not the shape of the code, and supplying the type is what narrows."
   :why-narrowing
   "The same functions carry three element types inside one program:
    datalog.core uses set/difference over sets of logic variables (symbols)
    AND over sets of result tuples, and kotobase.datom-plan does
    `(reduce set/union #{} quad-sets)` over sets of quad maps. One
    monomorphic copy per element type would be needed, and datalog.core
    passes set/union as a VALUE to merge-with, where there is no call site to
    monomorphise at."
   :second-blocker
   {:what :bounded-typed-sets
    :disposition :intentional
    :note "max-typed-set-items is 32 and :set-literal :language-semantics is
           :bounded-unique-typed-set -- the declared design, not a gap. A
           semi-naive datalog fixpoint's candidate sets are result sets.
           Recorded separately so a reader does not expect these modules to
           migrate the day element-type polymorphism lands."}
   :design-and-experiment "kotoba-lang lang/surface-status.edn :other-gaps
                           :element-type-polymorphism :design -- option C
                           (linker-instantiated module type parameters)
                           recommended and its kill conditions measured clear
                           2026-09-11; root ADR-2609113100"
   :not-the-blocker
   "Three surface details were measured and are NOT why this is blocked.
    Variadic: `&` is refused only inside a MULTI-ARITY declaration, and a
    single variadic clause is admitted, so union/difference/intersection can
    keep their whole arity surface in one clause. `every?`: not an admitted
    head, but a typed set is walkable with typed-set-nth and typed-set-count.
    `(set ...)`: not a builtin, and `into` is. Each of those is a spelling."}}

 ;; datom-source is not blocked as a whole -- after the protocol-docstring and
 ;; reader-metadata fixes its modules advance to :kotoba.error/protocol-method,
 ;; and measured 2026-09-11 that is two things: a multi-expression method body
 ;; (a spelling; `do` is admitted, `defn` has the same one-result rule) and,
 ;; in CountingSource, `swap!` on an atom held in a RECORD FIELD, which the
 ;; local-state slice refuses by design (`swap! expects a let-bound atom
 ;; cell`). The widening is already on the plan: surface-status :local-state
 ;; :widening-path :state-kit-desugar with its preconditions. CountingSource is
 ;; a call-counting diagnostic source; whether it belongs in the component or
 ;; in the oracle is the component author's call, not the compiler's.
 :blocked-definitions
 {:datom-source/CountingSource
  {:status :blocked :as-of "2026-09-11"
   :blocker :atom-in-record-field
   :blocker-record "kotoba-lang lang/surface-status.edn :local-state :widening-path"}}

